<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ardabil</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ardabil is a city in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. It was the home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safaviyya">Safaviyya Sufi order</a> founded by Sheikh Safi al-Din, whose shrine is located there. The order's leaders became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid dynasty</a>, and the city was the spiritual centre from which the Safavid Empire emerged.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ardabil.jpg" alt="Ardabil" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shayan imani · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sheikh_Safi_Addin_complex.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The ruling house was Sunni Muslim, but it showed favour to Sufi orders, and Uzun Hasan married his sister to Shaykh Junayd and his daughter to Junayd’s son Haydar, leaders of the Safavid order of <b>Ardabil</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Around them gathered the Saadlu, Karamanlu, Alpaut, Agacheri, Dukharlu, Jagirlu and Hajilu tribes, from Nakhchivan and Ganja to Maraş, Erzurum and <b>Ardabil</b>; their own traditions say they came to Anatolia from Central Asia with the Mongol conquest.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qara-qoyunlu">Qara Qoyunlu · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…Empire was founded in 1501 when the fourteen-year-old Ismail I, head of the Safaviyya Sufi order based at <b>Ardabil</b> in north-western Iran, led the Qizilbash, a confederation of Turkmen tribes (traditionally seven) named for their distinctive red headgear, to victory over the Aq Qoyunlu at the Battle of…</blockquote><a href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The end of the Shirvanshah state was bound up with the rise of the Safavid order of <b>Ardabil</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/shirvanshah-state">Shirvanshah State · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Tajlu Khanum, Ismail I’s queen, financed the Golden Iwan of the Fatima Masumeh Shrine at Qom in 1519, built her husband’s tomb at <b>Ardabil</b> and supported Tahmasp’s accession in 1524.</blockquote><a href="/societies/safavid-iran">Social Hierarchy of Safavid Iran</a></li><li><blockquote>Near <b>Ardabil</b> in north-western Iran, a Khazar army led by Barjik destroyed the forces of the Umayyad Caliphate and killed their commander, al-Jarrah ibn Abdallah al-Hakami.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-9">9 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
